---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHeader from "../../components/PageHeader.astro";
import DynamicsEngine from "../../novascope/components/DynamicsEngine.astro";

/*
 * Thin, like /explore/census: the page owns the copy and the framing, the engine
 * owns the interaction. /dynamics-lab is a 762-line monolith, which is exactly
 * why nothing could be reused from it.
 *
 * This page shows segregation EMERGING: the ICs place stars without regard to
 * mass (`segregation: 0`), so the arrangement has to be earned by integrating
 * gravity, and that is the whole claim.
 *
 * It used to point at /explore/mass-segregation as the primordial counterpart.
 * That page is quarantined pending a redesign, so the paragraph stands on its
 * own — which it should have done anyway: "this is earned" is verifiable here,
 * from this page's own initial conditions, without a second page to contrast it
 * against.
 */
---

<BaseLayout
  title="No star is an island — Anna Rosen"
  description="Turn gravity on and watch a star cluster come alive: the heaviest stars sink, the cluster segregates, and none of it was put in by hand."
  path="/explore/dynamics"
>
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Explore · interactive" title="No star is an island">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      Until now a cluster has been a list of stars. Switch gravity on and it becomes a
      <em>system</em>: every star pulls on every other, energies get shuffled by close
      passages, and the heaviest stars give up energy and sink toward the centre. Nothing
      below is choreographed — <strong>mass segregation is not a rule that was added</strong>,
      it is what this same gravity does when you let it run.
    </Fragment>
  </PageHeader>

  <div class="dyn-page wrap-wide">
    <DynamicsEngine />

    <div class="dyn-page__note prose wrap">
      <p>
        Press run with <strong>no gravity</strong> selected. Every star keeps the velocity it
        was born with and flies off in a straight line, and within a few million years there
        is no cluster left — nothing was ever holding it together. Now press
        <strong>gravity</strong>: the run restarts from the same initial conditions and keeps
        going. The same stars, the same velocities, one term added — and now they hold.
      </p>
      <p>
        With gravity on, the two curves in the right-hand panel start together at 1 and then
        part — the heaviest stars contracting <em>below</em> where they began while the
        lightest climb away. That is a measurement rather than an impression: the same stars
        are followed all run, so the curves cannot separate by relabelling who counts as heavy.
        Each is drawn relative to its own starting radius, because the heaviest 10% is a small
        enough group that its absolute radius is a noisy number; the two absolute radii are in
        the readout, and at the start they differ by sampling alone.
      </p>
      <p>
        A gap between those curves is not by itself the evidence, and it is worth seeing why.
        Run it with gravity <strong>off</strong> and the two still separate — by about 20%
        within eight crossing times, and then they hold there, because the heavy and light
        groups simply start at different radii and with different speeds. What gravity does is
        different in kind rather than in size: the heavy curve drops <em>below</em> 1 while the
        light one climbs away. That crossing is the segregation; the offset is not.
      </p>
      <p>
        Watch the energy strip along the bottom while gravity is on. It plots the fractional
        energy error against the tolerance the run is checked at, and for the first several
        crossing times it sits around 10<sup>−9</sup> — the integrator is barely working.
        Then it climbs, in <em>steps</em>. Each step is not a generic "close encounter": it is
        one specific thing, and the panel above will tell you when it happens.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The cluster builds a binary, and then the binary breaks the arithmetic.</strong>
        Segregation sinks the heaviest stars to the centre, where two of them capture each other
        — and a hard binary <em>hardens</em>, giving up energy to every star that passes it. On
        the draw I measured, the pair's orbit tightened from about 20,000 integrator steps per
        orbit to under 100 while its binding energy went from 3 to roughly 400 times a typical
        star's kinetic energy. Once an orbit takes only tens of steps, a fixed step cannot follow
        it through closest approach, and the error jumps. It then sits flat until the next jump —
        which is exactly what a symplectic scheme should do: the error arrives in discrete events
        and does not creep between them.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the strip is measuring two things at once, and it is worth keeping them apart. The
        <em>steps</em> are real cluster physics — binaries form and harden, and that is a result,
        not a bug. The <em>height</em> of them is a statement about this browser's arithmetic. On
        some draws the trace crosses the limit; when it does, the number turns red and the run has
        stopped being trustworthy from that point on, whatever the picture above it looks like.
        With gravity off the strip stays empty, because there is no integrator running to make a
        claim about.
      </p>
      <p>
        Drop <strong>N</strong> and the curves get noisier: with few stars, segregation is a
        statement about an average that barely has enough members to be an average. This is
        the same sampling problem the <a href="/explore/census">census</a> shows at the
        high-mass end, arriving from the other direction.
      </p>
      <p class="dyn-page__limits">
        <strong>What this model does and does not know.</strong> The integrator is a symplectic
        scheme of the same family my <code>gravax</code> code uses, run here on a few hundred
        stars so it keeps up with a browser; gravity is softened at small separations
        (ε = 0.1 r<sub>h</sub> N<sup>−1/3</sup>, about 0.009 pc at N = 400), so very close
        encounters are smoothed rather than resolved.
      </p>
      <p class="dyn-page__limits">
        The stars <em>move</em> but they do not <em>age</em>: masses, temperatures and
        luminosities are ZAMS values held fixed for the whole run. That is a limit on the
        dynamics and not only on the picture. The most massive star in a typical draw here is
        about 39 M<sub>☉</sub>, which would leave the main sequence within roughly 5 Myr and
        return most of its mass — but this run keeps integrating it at its birth mass. Past a few
        tens of Myr the cluster you are watching is a cluster of stars that would no longer exist,
        and the energy strip cannot tell you that: a conserved energy says the integrator solved
        the equations it was given, not that those equations still describe a star cluster.
      </p>
      <p class="dyn-page__limits">
        The segregation you are watching is <em>earned</em>: the initial conditions place stars
        without regard to mass, so nothing here arranges the heavy ones and the arrangement has
        to come from the gravity you switched on.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .dyn-page {
    padding-block: var(--space-l) var(--space-3xl);
  }
  .dyn-page__note {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2xl);
  }
  .dyn-page__limits {
    padding-block-start: var(--space-s);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--border);
    color: var(--ink-dim);
  }
</style>
